<title>PAKISTAN: PAKISTAN NEW CROP COTTON HOLDS STEADY.</title>
<headline>PAKISTAN NEW CROP COTTON HOLDS STEADY.</headline>
<dateline>KARACHI, Pakistan 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Pakistani new crop cotton closed steady on Tuesday amid balanced demand and supply, dealers at the private Karachi Cotton Association (KCA) said.</p>
<p>Textile mills were the main buyers of the new crop, while exporters were tilting towards the cheaper old crop, dealer Nasim Usman said. The supply position, disrupted by monsoon rains, was likely to improve in the next few days, he added.</p>
<p>In the kerb market, new crop of NIAB-78 variety was quoted at 2,580/2,590 rupees per maund (37.32 kg).  </p>
<p>The KCA said exporters had shipped 142,212 bales of cotton by July 19 compared to 1.82 million at the same time a year ago. Exporters had registered contracts for the export of 190,080 bales by July 28 compared to 2.32 million a year ago.</p>
<p>KCA's official spot rates in rupees per maund:</p>
<p>				     Tuesday	    August 11</p>
<p>    NIAB-78 (1997/98 crop)   2,588.13	   2,588.13</p>
<p>    K-68    (1996/97 crop)   2,554.38	   2,554.38</p>
<p>    MNH-93  (1996/97 crop)   2,558.75	   2,558.75</p>
<p>($1=40.62 rupees)</p>
